+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSocket delivery of board updates
+
+## Context
+
+Editors submit change commands over HTTPS to one authoritative board per event, and each committed edit receives one event revision. Committed revisions must reach up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, with opening-time bursts around 20 edits per second, and a browser that drops offline for up to 20 minutes must be able to recover the latest board on return. The delivery route — how committed revisions travel from server to browser — shapes three coupled components: the change store (what read indexes and publication guarantees it needs), the delivery component (what it owns), and browser sync (subscription, reconnect, and catch-up behavior). A browser that silently shows a stale board is the primary failure mode to avoid: a disconnected browser must be distinguishable from an unchanged one.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Gates (all options must pass):
+
+- **Freshness target.** Committed changes visible in connected browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity. All three delivery routes in the domain clear this; it is a floor, not a discriminator.
+- **Authoritative revision.** Whatever reaches the browser must be the server-assigned event revision, never a browser-side merge. All routes satisfy this via the store; it is inherited from the tenet that a committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of delivery route.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Disconnect visibility (major).** A browser cut off from updates must be able to tell it is cut off, not render a quietly stale board. Derived from the tenet that connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board.
+- **Recovery correctness under disconnect (major).** A browser returning after up to 20 minutes offline must converge on the latest board without gaps or duplicates. This is a stated project goal, and it demands some replay-ordered read of missed revisions no matter which route delivers the live feed.
+- **Recurring cost (moderate).** The pilot runs on a small budget; ongoing per-month fees weigh more than one-time implementation effort.
+- **Vendor coupling (moderate).** A single-update-stream board has little to gain from a provider's feature surface, and a provider outage, pricing change, or credential failure becomes an outage of the board's core function.
+- **Operational burden (moderate).** Connection capacity, reconnect storms, and process supervision are real work if self-owned. Neither minimum operations nor minimum cost was declared the overriding criterion, so this trades against the others rather than dominating.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers run a loop that re-requests board changes every five seconds. The store exposes cursor queries — "give me everything since revision N" — and each poll response is a plain request/response. No connection state exists on the server: any process can answer any poll, restarts are invisible, and capacity scales with ordinary HTTP handling.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Gates: **passes** both — five seconds is well inside the 15-second target, and revisions are read directly from the store.
+- Disconnect visibility: **weak** — a stalled network makes a poll simply not return; distinguishing "no changes" from "no contact" needs a separate heartbeat discipline bolted on.
+- Recovery correctness: **partial** — cursor replay converges correctly on reconnect, but a missed poll interval can present a stale board during a silent outage.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — request/response only; no new infrastructure.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — nothing beyond the HTTPS boundary the project already runs.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — no sessions to supervise; the loop is browser-side.
+- **Summary.** Simplest to operate, at the price of continuous redundant reads and ambiguity between silence and disconnection.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The project runs its own socket endpoint. The delivery component accepts browser connections, tracks session state, and publishes each committed revision to all connected sessions as it commits. The store retains replayable revisions, so a browser that reconnects asks for everything since its last received revision and replays forward. Fastest updates of the three: push on commit, no loop, no wait interval.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Gates: **passes** both — push-on-commit is well inside the target, and replay uses the same authoritative revisions.
+- Disconnect visibility: **strong** — a dropped socket is an explicit, detectable event in the browser; the reconnect path is a designed behavior, not an afterthought.
+- Recovery correctness: **strong** — reconnect-plus-replay over ordered revisions converges without gaps, and the same store mechanism serves both live publication and recovery.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no per-message or per-connection fees; cost is the server capacity the project already budgets.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — no external provider; the update path stays inside the project's own HTTPS infrastructure.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity, session supervision, reconnect-storm handling at opening time, and orderly shutdowns. This is the accepted cost of this option.
+- **Summary.** Fast, visible, provider-free updates in exchange for owning connection operations outright.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime provider (push service) delivers updates. The server publishes committed revisions to the provider's API and reconciles publication success or failure; browsers subscribe through the provider's client library, which carries reconnection and catch-up semantics. The provider operates the connection fleet and edge presence, so the project's own operations surface stays small.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Gates: **passes** both, contingent on the provider meeting the freshness target — reasonable for any credible realtime vendor.
+- Disconnect visibility: **partial** — the provider's client surfaces connection state, but the failure mode becomes partly the provider's behavior, observed rather than designed.
+- Recovery correctness: **partial** — provider catch-up covers common disconnects, but the store still needs publication reconciliation (did the provider actually get every revision?) to be sure no revision is lost between server and provider.
+- Recurring cost: **weak** — recurring fees scale with connections or messages; 800 browsers across event hours is a standing bill.
+- Vendor coupling: **weak** — provider credentials, API limits, pricing changes, and outages sit inside the board's core update path, and the store's publication logic is shaped around the provider's semantics.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — the provider carries connection capacity and reconnect mechanics; the project wraps rather than runs them.
+- **Summary.** Lowest-latency updates with the least connection operations, bought with fees and a dependency in the core path.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** It is the only option strong on both major criteria at once: disconnect visibility is a designed socket behavior rather than a heartbeat bolted onto polling, and recovery correctness comes from the same replayable revisions that live publication uses — one store mechanism, not two. The moderate criteria break its way too: no recurring fees and no provider inside the update path, which matters because the board's update stream is a single, narrow feed that a vendor's feature surface cannot enrich. The cost is accepted up front: the project owns connection capacity, session supervision, and reconnect-storm handling at opening time. This burden is bounded — 800 simultaneous browsers is a modest fleet — and it is the trade the tenets point toward: connectivity failures must be visible, and self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted, so the choice turns on the major criteria rather than on any prohibition.
+
+Committing to this option forecloses the simplest stateless operation: polling's any-process-can-serve property is gone, and delivery is now a stateful component. It obligates downstream: the change store must keep replayable revisions and a publication path delivery can rely on; the delivery component owns socket sessions and reliable publication; browser sync owns reconnect and catch-up over the socket.
